@keithamus 

> Chrome removes implicit anchor association during "remove from the top layer immediately". So it would make sense for it to be step 12, not 9.5.

That would still be too early for the codepen demo, right? It'd result in the Safari behaviour.

> [searchfox.org/wubkat/source/Source/WebCore/html/HTMLElement.cpp#1230](https://searchfox.org/wubkat/source/Source/WebCore/html/HTMLElement.cpp#1230) - WebKit might actually be removing the implicit anchor too early regardless, I think this line needs moving down to 1245 too. Doing that locally the behaviour changes but it's no better (the popover moves to the vertical bottom but horizontal center).

https://searchfox.org/wubkat/source/Source/WebCore/dom/Element.cpp#3280 this is where the "bug" lies in WebKit; this `invalidateStyleInternal()` would need the equivalent guard:

```cpp
void Element::setInvokedPopover(RefPtr<Element>&& element)
{
    auto& data = ensureElementRareData();
    auto* hadAnchor = data.getInvokedPopover();
    data.setInvokedPopover(WTFMove(element));

    if (!hadAnchor) {
	    // Invalidate so isPopoverInvoker style bit gets updated.
	    invalidateStyleInternal();
    }
}
```

This would behave more like Chrome, but I think it's fragile. I think it would be preferred to detect a transition and queue removal _after_ the animation/transition cycle.

@lukewarlow 

> So I think this does need a spec change, at the very least implicit anchor element should only be set to null once https://drafts.csswg.org/css-position-4/#remove-an-element-from-the-top-layer-immediately finishes (be that called directly or via the request mechanism).

It'd be nice if this gets spec'd as a callback, so the removal happens in the HTML spec, since that's where it's set.
